    "title": "Referee Brutally Truck-Sticks Coach Along The Sideline [VIDEO] - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Darrelle Lincoln ,\n4 hours ago\nReferee trucks coach. (Photo via Twitter) This is why people coaches always have someone behind them to get them out of the way when a referee is patrolling the sidelines.\nOne coach found out the hard way tonight.\nArkansas State hosted South Alabama tonight in an important Sun Belt Conference football matchup at Centennial Bank Stadium. Both teams entered the game looking to establish momentum in the highly competitive West Division race.\nWith the game tied at 7, South Alabama was looking to break that tie with a long play near the end of the first quarter.\nSide Judge Referee Delivers Brutal Block on Coach Before Touchdown A side judge accidentally leveled someone standing along the sideline. The official was running fast to track a 100-yard kickoff touchdown return. Consequently, he completely plowed over a person near the boundary line.\nIt didn’t even appear that the coach was paying attention to what was happening on the field. It wasn’t until the referee knocked him over that he realized he was in the way.\nNot only did South Alabama score, but the coach took quite a hit and tumble to the ground.\nAs of this writing, both teams have scored a lot in this game as South Alabama currently leads early in the third quarter. 41 points were scored between the teams in the second quarter alone.\nThe post Referee Brutally Truck-Sticks Coach Along The Sideline [VIDEO] originally published on Total Pro Sports .",
    "summary": "(Photo via Twitter) This is why people coaches always have someone behind them to get them out of the way when a referee is patrolling the sidelines. Arkansas State hosted South Alabama tonight in an important Sun Belt Conference football matchup at Centennial Bank Stadium. Both teams entered the game looking to establish momentum in the highly competitive West Division race. As of this writing, both teams have scored a lot in this game as South Alabama currently leads early in the third quarter.",
